mwan3 reserves route and rule protocol 201 and the mwan3_ firewall namespace.

Route copies strip the source protocol and are added exclusively with protocol
201. Updates withdraw only a matching owned route before adding its replacement.
Numeric route types are converted to the native ip route keywords before
snapshot copying, event handling and owned-route replacement/deletion. This
preserves reject routes without treating their numeric type as an IP prefix.
Stop, reload and interface withdrawal select the protocol, including IPv6 and
old tables no longer present in UCI; they do not flush numeric table/rule ranges.
Startup refuses occupied routing tables/priorities and a preexisting unclaimed
firewall namespace. Untagged state from older releases must be explicitly
removed by its administrator before this release starts; it is not adopted.

Other route producers must use their own protocol and tables. Protocol 201 is
an ownership namespace, not protection against another privileged process
intentionally impersonating mwan3. Use normal procd lifecycle commands when
changing configuration so old rules are withdrawn before IDs are reassigned.

Monitor and hotplug writers share the native procd lock with teardown. A service
incarnation receipt is retired under that lock before kernel resources are
withdrawn; events queued by an old monitor cannot modify a later incarnation.
For each address family, an installed mwan3_iface_in_ chain identifies a live
interface routing owner. Shared connected-route updates reach that owner even
when it has no probes or its tracker is restarting. Probe health selects uplink
policy; it does not suspend route maintenance. Interface withdrawal removes the
chain under the same lock, so later events cannot recreate its retired table.

The route monitor verifies its subscribed netlink socket before the initial dump.
Teardown retains a retired ownership receipt on cleanup failure; a later stop
retries it, and start withdraws retired resources before claiming a new owner.
